course: dictionary
lesson: 12688
title: Paths that split: the word divergence
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Divergence is a word for things that start together and then go further apart, like two paths splitting at a fork. When you meet it, look for the clues around it. «At the fork in the woods, the two paths began to show a clear divergence.» The words fork and two paths tell you the paths are separating, so divergence means going apart.
explain: Divergence can also mean people thinking differently. «The class had a divergence of opinion about the new playground.» If people have a divergence of opinion, they disagree or see things in different ways. Ask yourself: is this about things moving apart, or about people who do not agree? The sentence will tell you which.
explain: In maths and science, divergence means a growing difference. «On the graph, the two lines showed a divergence: one rose while the other fell.» The lines were once close and are now moving further apart. The verb form is diverge, and the adjective form is divergent, as in «divergent paths».
fact: Divergence is a noun. It means going further apart, or a growing difference.
fact: The verb diverge means to go further apart and in different directions.
fact: The adjective divergent describes things that are moving apart or differ from each other.
fact: A divergence of opinion means people disagree or think differently.
fact: In maths and science, divergence means a growing difference between numbers, lines or results.
exercise: «At the fork in the woods, the two paths began to show a clear divergence.» What does divergence mean here? | Getting narrower and darker | Stopping for a rest | *Going further apart in different directions | Coming together into one path
exercise: «The class had a divergence of opinion about the new playground.» What does divergence of opinion mean? | The playground was closed | *The children had different views | The class went outside to play | The children all agreed
exercise: Which word is the verb form of divergence? | divert | divergences | *diverge | divergent
test: «On the graph, the two lines showed a divergence: one rose while the other fell.» In maths or science, what is a divergence? | A graph with no numbers | Two lines that are exactly the same | *A growing difference between the two lines | A line drawn in a straight row
test: Which phrase means people disagree or think differently? | *Divergence of opinion | Point of agreement | Unity of purpose | Shared plan
exam: Which word means things that start together and then go further apart, like paths that split? | Famished | *Divergence | Drooped | Curt
